course: dictionary
lesson: 15867
title: Ungrammatical sentences
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A sentence is ungrammatical when it does not follow the rules of grammar, the rules for how words fit together. «She go to school yesterday» is ungrammatical because go should be went, since yesterday tells us it already happened.
explain: Think of it as a broken rule, not a broken word. Every word in a sentence can be a real word, but it may be in the wrong form or the wrong order. «Dog the big ran» uses real words in the wrong order, so it is ungrammatical too.
explain: Be careful: some speech breaks Standard English rules but still follows the rules of a dialect. «We was late» is ungrammatical in Standard English, even though many people say it. In a test or in your writing, use Standard English.
explain: Linguists mark an ungrammatical sentence with a star, like this: *She go to school yesterday. The star is a signal to the reader. It is not part of the sentence.
fact: Ungrammatical means not following the rules of grammar.
fact: «She go to school yesterday» is ungrammatical because go should be went.
fact: «We was» and «I ain't» are counted as ungrammatical in Standard English, even though they follow the rules of some dialects.
fact: In linguistics, an ungrammatical sentence is marked with a star (*).
fact: A quick check: read the sentence aloud and ask whether the words are in the right form and the right order.
exercise: Which sentence is ungrammatical? | *She go to school yesterday. | They are playing outside. | The cat sat on the mat.
exercise: Which correction fixes «She go to school yesterday»? | She goes to school yesterday | She going to school yesterday | Yesterday school she to went | *She went to school yesterday
exercise: What does ungrammatical mean? | Too long to read | Written in a foreign language | *Not following the rules of grammar | Very funny
test: Which word is wrong in «The dogs runs fast»? | The | fast | dogs | *runs
test: Which is correct in Standard English? | *I haven't got any | I ain't got none | We was going | Him is my friend
exam: Which sentence is ungrammatical? | *Me and him goes home now. | The sun is bright today. | The boys played football. | Yesterday we walked to the park.
